          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong Sasakian manifold
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contact+manifold"} contact manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,\theta)
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} metric
            such that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone+%28Riemannian+geometry%29"} Riemannian cone
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifold"} Kähler manifold
          %p
            If the manifold is also an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+manifold"} Einstein manifold
            one speaks of
            = succeed "." do
              %em Sasaki-Einstein manifolds
          %p
            When the Riemannian cone is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyper-K%C3%A4hler+manifold"} hyper-Kähler manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %strong 3-Sasakian manifold
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            An example is an odd-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∖
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{2n-1} \subset \mathbb{C}^n\setminus\{\mathbf{0}\}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifold"} complex manifold
            carries its standard Kähler structure.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            When the Riemannian cone is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyper-K%C3%A4hler+manifold"} hyper-Kähler manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %strong 3-Sasakian manifold
          %p
            Such a manifold is then automatically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+manifold"} Einstein
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin
          %p
            A 7-dimensional 3-Sasakian manifold carries a 1-parameter family of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82+manifold"} structures
            (see e.g. (
            %a{:href => "#AgFried10"}> Agricola-Friedrich 2010
            )).
